-- Generated by scripts/pages.ts from i18n/frFR/recipes/: edit those, not this file.
-- pages[chef][spell] = { paragraph, ... }: the chef's page of each dish.
local _, ns = ...
ns.pages = {
  kendor = {
    [2538] = { "Every cook begins here, with a fire, a knife and a wolf that did not want to be dinner. Most of them never move on, which is how the roads of this kingdom came to smell of burnt wolf. Charring is not burning. You want the outside dark and crisp and the inside still yielding, and that means a fire that has settled to coals, not one still showing off its flames. Learn this, and you have learned patience. Then come and see me." },
    [2539] = { "The second dish every trainer teaches, and the first one most cooks get wrong. They believe spice is a disguise. They take a wolf that has run itself lean in the hills and bury it, and the wolf deserved better. Rub the spice in before the meat meets the fire, then leave it to settle. Spice added at the end sits on the meat like a hat. Spice added at the start becomes part of it." },
    [2540] = { "Elwynn's boars have fed this city since before it had walls, and they will feed it after the walls need rebuilding again. Tomas in Goldshire roasts them whole for his inn, turning the spit by hand and calling it tradition. It is a fine tradition. It is also why his boar is done on one side and hopeful on the other. Cut the meat into even pieces, so the heat treats them all alike. Fairness, in a kitchen, is a matter of knife work." },
    [2541] = { "The coyotes of Westfall eat whatever the farmers left behind, and the meat tastes of a hard life. You cannot change its past. You can respect it. Cut the steak thick, sear it hard, and take it off the heat while the middle still blushes. A thin coyote steak cooked through is a sandal. A thick one, rested, is supper. Every apprentice arrives thinking a steak is a simple thing. It is. That is why it is so easy to ruin." },
    [2546] = { "Dry ribs are a farmer's dish, a boar's ribs and a rub of spice with no sauce at all. Some cooks are frightened of that. They believe a rib must drip to be good. A rib must be cooked to be good. Rub the spice in the night before, let the fire die down to a low glow, and give the ribs the whole afternoon. They come out dark, crisp and dry to the touch, and they leave nothing on the bone." },
    [2548] = { "The gnolls of Redridge carry this recipe around in their packs, stolen from some farmhouse, and do nothing with it. I sell the same recipe in Stormwind and do a great deal with it. Two racks of boar ribs and hot spice, and the whole secret is the turning. Turn them every few minutes, so the fat bastes the meat as it renders. Succulent is not a promise in the name. It is a reward for attention." },
    [2549] = { "This is the dish I would put my name to, and I have. Lean wolf flank and Stormwind Seasoning Herbs, nothing else, and anyone who offers you a substitute for the herbs is selling something. Leave a gap between the pieces on the skewer, so the heat reaches every side. Rest it before you eat it. A kabob rushed off the fire weeps on the plate, and so will you." },
    [2795] = { "Ragnar Thunderbrew believes this recipe belongs to his family. I sell it in Stormwind at a fair price, and I have improved it. He will tell you the ribs need Rhapsody Malt from the first minute to the last. They do not. The dwarves baste from the start and then wonder why the outside burns while the inside sulks. Roast the rib bare until the fat runs clear, and only then begin the malt, a little at a time, so it turns to a glaze instead of a char. The result is sweet, dark and sticky, and it is the finest thing to come out of Dun Morogh. I would tell Ragnar so, if he could hear anything over his own tavern. I suspect he knows. His ribs have improved since." },
    [3370] = { "Crocolisk is the steak for those who think beef too obliging. The Wetlands are full of them, and the hunters of Menethil bring me the tails in barrels of brine. The meat is pale and firm, closer to fish than to beast. Cook it as you would a thick fish: hot, short, and never twice. Reheated crocolisk tastes of the swamp it came from, which is a taste no one has ever asked for." },
    [3371] = { "Thelsamar's dwarves make blood sausage every winter and send a few to Ironforge, where Daryl Riknussun praises them loudly and then teaches his students to make them worse. I sell the recipe here, corrected. Bear meat, cleaned intestines and spider ichor, which binds as blood once did and keeps far better. Stuff the casing loosely. A tight sausage splits in the pan and empties itself onto the coals, and no one has ever loved a sausage that gave up." },
    [3397] = { "The bears of Ashenvale grow larger than ours, on a diet of night elf patience and whatever they find under the trees. Their meat is dark and rich and wants a hot spice to cut it. Sear it hard and let it rest a long while, longer than a smaller animal, because a big steak keeps cooking after it leaves the fire. In Desolace, I hear, a goblin machine sells my recipes one copy at a time. I would like to meet its cook." },
    [3398] = { "Lions of the Barrens, cut into chops and burned with hot spice by cooks who call the result a delicacy. I call it a lion that deserved better. A lion's meat is lean and sweet close to the bone, and the chop should be cooked on the bone for that reason. Hot spice is welcome. Hot fire is not. Give the chops a moderate heat and turn them often. A lion should arrive at the table looking as if it lost a fair fight." },
    [3399] = { "Tasty, it calls itself, and it is too modest by half. Two cuts of lion and soothing spice, cooked as a steak rather than chopped, which is the only civilised way to eat a lion. The soothing spice is not there to calm the cook. It rounds the meat's sweetness. Sear it, rest it, slice it across the grain. A lion steak sliced along the grain chews like an argument you cannot win." },
    [4094] = { "In the Badlands they will tell you a buzzard is not worth the coals. They have not eaten it barbecued. A buzzard wing is mostly sinew and spite, so it wants hot spice and a long, low heat, then a short, fierce one at the end to blister the skin. The hunters of Arathi sell the recipe for a pittance, which is a crime against cooking, and I have reported it to no one." },
    [6412] = { "The night elves of Teldrassil will teach you this kabob for the price of a few spider legs, which tells you what they think of spiders. Alegorn in Darnassus would have you thread each leg with a sprig of something green and arrange the skewers like a poem. Ignore the poem. Spider legs want a fierce, short heat, enough to crack the shell and no more, or the meat inside turns to string. Then you may garnish, if you must. I never must." },
    [6413] = { "A dish of the Horde, from the dust of Durotar, and the surprise is that anyone eats a scorpid on purpose. I have no need to taste the cooking of Orgrimmar. I have, however, read the recipe, because a master reads everything. Its one wisdom is the stinger. Cooked until it turns from glass to chalk, it loses its venom and keeps its bite. Undercooked, it keeps both. I leave you to decide which of those you would call a surprise." },
    [6414] = { "Kodo is the meat of Mulgore, roasted there by tauren who cook under open sky with the plains for a kitchen. I have never stood under that sky, and I have no plans to. A kodo is an enormous, patient animal, and its meat is patient too: tough at first, generous at the end. Roast it slowly and rest it as long as it roasted. The tauren are right about one thing, and it is this: some meat cannot be hurried by anyone." },
    [6418] = { "A Barrens dish, sold at the Crossroads and eaten by the Horde with their fingers. I mention the fingers only so that you do not imitate them. A thunder lizard's tail is all muscle, and the crisp comes from cooking it in its own fat until the skin shatters. Hot spice, a hot pan, and a cook brave enough to leave it there. I will not pretend to admire the Barrens. I will admit the tail is well conceived." },
    [6419] = { "Venison is the meat of a cook with good manners. A stag is lean and proud and must be treated as such, with plenty of spice rubbed in and very little time on the fire. Overcook a stag and you have insulted the forest. The elves of Ashenvale sell this recipe, which I find touching, since they spend so much of their lives keeping anyone from hunting their stags. Cook it rare, slice it thin, and say nothing about where it came from." },
    [8607] = { "Smoking is the dwarves' art, and I say so without pleasure. In Loch Modan they hang bear meat in the smoke of their chimneys and forget it there through the winter, and by spring it is better than anything they meant to cook. The secret is distance. The meat hangs high, where the heat never reaches and only the smoke does the work. Close to the fire, you are roasting badly. Far from it, you are smoking well." },
    [15853] = { "A plain steak from the flank of a wolf, and plainness is the hardest thing to cook. There is no sauce to hide behind and no stew to bury a mistake in. The flank is lean, so it must be seared fast and rested long, with a little spice to remind it that it is food. Rare is correct. Medium is forgivable. Beyond that, I suggest a different trade, and I know several that need no fire at all." },
    [15855] = { "Roast raptor is soldiers' food, sold at the camps in Stranglethorn where men eat what tried to eat them. That is reason enough to respect it. Raptor flesh is lean and quick, so it roasts best in large pieces that protect their own middles. Hot spice on the outside, a strong fire, and then away from the heat to finish. A raptor roasted like a chicken comes out like a boot. A raptor roasted like a raptor comes out like a victory." },
    [15856] = { "The wolves of the far south are red, ill-tempered and larger than ours, and their ribs come with a great deal of meat attached. Feralas sells this recipe to anyone passing through, which few do. Hot spice, a hot fire, and then patience, because red wolf is fattier than our grey kind and needs time to render. When the fat runs clear and the bones turn dark, they are done. When the fat smokes, so is your reputation." },
    [15863] = { "Carrion Surprise. I have read the recipe twice, in case the name was a joke. It is not. Mystery meat and hot spice, cooked into a dish that the trolls of the Swamp of Sorrows sell as though it were a kindness. The meat must be cooked through and then cooked through again, because whatever it once was, it has been dead a while. I do not serve this. I teach it, so that my pupils know what they are avoiding." },
    [15935] = { "This recipe comes from Tirisfal, where the cooks are dead and the bats are not, yet. It reached me through a merchant I trust more than his goods. I will grant it this: a wing is mostly skin, and skin rewards a cook who leaves it alone. Lay it on a hot iron, skin down, and do not touch it until it lets go of the pan on its own. That is the whole art. The rest of Tirisfal's cooking I leave to Tirisfal." },
    [21175] = { "White spider meat, pressed into a sausage and taught by every cooking trainer in the land as though it were ordinary. It is not ordinary. Spider meat is sweet and delicate, closer to crab than to pork, and it takes no spice because it needs none. The art is the casing: firm enough to hold, thin enough to crack in the pan. Many pupils pale at the word spider. I ask them whether they have ever eaten a crab, and wait." },
    [22480] = { "Tender wolf meat comes from the wolves of Winterspring and the far cold places, where an animal lives slowly and keeps its fat. It is the best wolf meat in the world, and it is wasted on most cooks. Soothing spice, a short hot sear, and then rest it as long as you cooked it. A cook who has ruined a hundred stringy wolves in Elwynn will finally understand, on this one, what they were reaching for." },
    [25659] = { "Dirge Quikcleave runs a kitchen in Gadgetzan, in the middle of a desert, where he cooks chimaerok with goblin rocket fuel and charges for the privilege. I have every reason to despise this recipe. I cannot. Chimaerok tenderloin is the finest meat on Kalimdor, dense and sweet, from beasts so far away that only the brave or the hungry ever bring it back. Deeprock salt from the dwarves' mines, hot spice, and the fuel, which does not flavour the meat so much as flatter the fire into a heat no honest coal can reach. Use a great deal of distance and very little of the fuel. Dirge would tell you otherwise. Dirge has fewer eyebrows than I do. The chops come out charred and juicy and, I admit it, the best thing a goblin ever made." },
    [1225758] = { "Prowler steak is tiger, which the name is too polite to say. Two cuts of it, rubbed with holy salts and left an hour before the fire touches them. Tiger is a lean, hard-working meat, and the salt tames it. The rest is restraint: a hot iron, a short sear and a long rest. I have heard hunters argue that a tiger should be cooked angry. Hunters should hunt, and leave the kitchen to those who can count to sixty." },
    [1225759] = { "Filet o' Flank, a name with a letter missing and a dish with nothing missing at all. The red wolf flank is rubbed with holy salts, which the clergy will tell you are blessed and the merchants will tell you are expensive. They are both right. The salt draws the water from the meat and firms it, so cook it quickly. A blessed steak overcooked is still overcooked. The Light will not save it, and neither will I." },
    [1250142] = { "Sliders, I am told, are small and many, which is how young cooks like everything. The stag is chopped fine with hot spice and pressed into rounds the size of a palm. Press them once and never again: a slider pressed twice gives its juice to the pan instead of the guest. I do not approve of food designed to be eaten standing. I approve of these, standing, and I am not proud of it." },
    [1250143] = { "A pamplona is meat flattened, filled and rolled, and the soaring one is made of buzzard, which is the only thing about it that soars. Three wings, boned and beaten flat, rolled around soothing spice and tied. It is fiddly work, and fiddly work is where a cook is tested. Roll it tight, roast it gently, and slice it into rounds. The rounds show your knife work. Make sure they show something you would sign." },
    [1250144] = { "Hachee is an old farmhouse word for a slow stew of chopped meat, and here the meat is bat. I would not have chosen bat. Bat would not have chosen me. Three wings, chopped, with soothing spice, and a low heat for most of an evening, until the sinew softens into something you could serve a guest. Whoever invented it was poor and clever. I respect the clever, and I have learned, over the years, to forgive the poor." },
    [1250146] = { "Au poivre means with pepper, in some foreign kitchen, and there is no pepper in it. There is red wolf flank and soothing spice, and the spice does the pepper's work well enough that no one complains. Sear the flank hard, then let it rest while the pan cools, and pour the juices back over it. Never carve it on the board where it seared. The board remembers the heat, and so will the meat." },
    [1250147] = { "Bruscitti. I have looked for the word in three languages and found it in none. The dish is plain enough to explain itself: big bear meat, sliced thin, layered with soothing spice and roasted slowly until the layers fuse. The trick is to slice it cold, because warm meat tears under the knife. Whoever named this dish was trying to sound foreign. The dish itself has nothing to hide, and I like it better than its name." },
    [1250148] = { "A stag steak served steaming, which is to say served at once, which is how every steak ought to be served and how almost none are. Three cuts of stag, soothing spice rubbed in deep, a very hot iron. The steam is the point. If it reaches the table without steam, it waited somewhere it should not have, and someone in your kitchen owes you an apology, and very likely a new steak." },
    [1250149] = { "A hunter's name for a cook's dish, as though the tiger had been caught by a thunderbolt. The tiger was caught by a hunter and a great deal of waiting. The steak, however, earns its name: three cuts of tiger, soothing spice, and the hottest fire you can make, for the shortest time you can bear. Strike fast, rest long. The second half is where most cooks lose their nerve." },
    [1250150] = { "A recipe I have heard described but never seen sold, which is the most dangerous kind. The raptor is long past raging by the time it reaches the pan, so the name must mean the spice, and there is a great deal of it. Cook the ribs low until the meat pulls from the bone, then over a fierce heat to set the crust. Serve them only to people who asked. I do not say this of every dish." },
    [1250151] = { "Brisket is the cut of a patient cook, and bear brisket is the cut of a saint. It is the toughest part of the animal, all hard work and old muscle. Rub it with hot spice and give it the lowest fire you can keep alight and the whole of a day. Somewhere in the evening it gives up and falls apart under a fork. I cannot tell you the hour. I can tell you that the cook who checks it too often never reaches it." },
    [1250152] = { "Prehistoric, as though the raptor had been waiting since the world began to be pulled apart with two forks. Perhaps it had. Three portions of raptor and a great deal of soothing spice, then the lowest fire you can tend for most of a day. The meat is done when it falls apart at a touch. Pull it with forks, never a knife. A knife makes it sliced raptor, and sliced raptor is a different and lesser book." },
    [1250153] = { "Rouladen is a dwarvish word, or a dwarvish claim on a word, and the dish is rolled meat: thin slices of raptor rolled around hot spice and tied. Rolled meat cooks from the outside in, so the outer turn must be thin or the middle stays raw. Brown the rolls on every side, then lower the heat and leave them be. When the string chars, cut it away. If you serve a guest the string, you deserve the look you get." },
    [1250154] = { "Twice spiced, and for once a dish tells the truth about itself. The raptor is rubbed once before it is sliced and once after it is cooked, and the second time is not for flavour but for the crust. Raptor flesh is lean and quick to dry. Slice it across the grain, thin enough to see a candle through, and give it the fire for a breath, no more. A recipe I did not invent, and would have, given time." },
    [1250156] = { "A recipe that turns up in the nests of raptors, which is either a joke or a warning. The bites are made from the egg, not the beast, and a raptor egg is a heavier, richer thing than any hen ever laid. Season it more generously than you think decent, because the yolk drinks every grain. Cook the bites small and quick. A raptor egg cooked slowly turns to rubber out of spite. I suspect the mother taught it that." },
    [1295834] = { "A recipe from the Riverglades, sold by a woman who saw no reason to give it a grander name, and she was right. Boar meat, a good deal of it, with a pinch of mild spice, roasted on the bone until the fat crackles. That is all. I have spent my life teaching cooks to resist doing more, and here is a farmer who never needed teaching. Rest it before you carve it. Plain food forgives nothing, which is why it is so seldom done well." },
  },
  eunice = {
    [2538] = { "The first dish of every cook, alive or not. You learn it on the road with a knife and a fire, because there is always a wolf and there is always someone hungry. Listen to it. Wolf meat hisses when it first meets the heat and falls quiet when the outside has sealed. Turn it once, at the quiet. My students want to poke it. I let them, once, and then I let them eat what they poked." },
    [2539] = { "I teach this on the second day, after the charred wolf, so that my students learn spice is not a correction. Rub it into the meat before the fire. When it goes on at the end, it smells of spice and of wolf, two strangers at the same table not speaking. When it goes on at the start, it smells of supper. The living tell me the difference is just as plain on the tongue." },
    [2540] = { "In Lordaeron we roasted boar at harvest, in a pit behind the barn, or behind the inn, or behind somebody's barn. I remember the pit clearly and the barn not at all. Down here it is a plain dish for plain days. Roast the meat in pieces, never whole: a whole boar takes the day, and the people waiting for it will not. When the fat begins to smell sweet instead of sharp, it is ready." },
    [2541] = { "Coyotes eat whatever the world drops, and their meat remembers every meal. It takes no spice, only a hot pan and a cook who does not apologise for it. Sear it until the edges curl and the smell turns from blood to toasted nuts, then take it off the heat and leave it alone. A coyote steak is never tender. It is honest, and honest food keeps a traveller on their feet for a long road." },
    [2546] = { "No sauce, only spice and a low fire and the whole of an afternoon. The ribs are done when the bones show a finger's width at the ends and the smell has gone from meat to something closer to toasted bread. I serve them on paper, to people who eat with their hands and do not mind who sees. In the Undercity, that is most people, and the rest are lying." },
    [2548] = { "Two racks of ribs and hot spice, turned every few minutes so the fat bastes the meat as it melts. The turning is the recipe. A rib left to itself burns on the bottom and sulks on top. A rib turned often comes out glossy and generous. I give this one to the students who think cooking is waiting. It is not. It is paying attention while you wait." },
    [2549] = { "Stormwind Seasoning Herbs, the packet says, and they smell exactly as I remember the market of the old capital smelling, which is to say I cannot tell you whether the memory is of Stormwind, or of Lordaeron, or of a dream. The wolf does not care. Thread the meat with gaps between the pieces, so the heat reaches every side, and listen for the fat to spit. When it stops spitting and starts to sing, turn the skewers." },
    [2795] = { "A dwarven dish, which means it comes with a story about whose grandfather invented it, and the story changes with whoever is telling it. I take no sides. The ribs and the malt are honest whoever cooked them first. Every dwarf I have heard of bastes from the first minute and then blames the fire for what follows. Roast the ribs bare until the fat runs clear, and only then begin the malt, a little at a time, so it turns to a glaze. When it smells of burnt sugar, you have gone too far. When it smells of bread and smoke, you are exactly right. The deathguards ask for this one by name, which is more than they do for their sergeants. I make it on feast days, and on the days that need one." },
    [3370] = { "Crocolisk reaches the Undercity from the Wetlands by way of people who would rather not say how. I do not ask. The meat is pale and firm, more fish than beast, and it wants a pinch of spice and very little time. Cook it once. Crocolisk heated a second time smells of the swamp, and the swamp is not what anyone ordered. Serve it at once, to whoever is standing nearest." },
    [3371] = { "Every autumn the farmhouses of Lordaeron made blood sausage, and every autumn someone's grandmother knew the only proper way. Mine swore by thyme. Or sage. I was certain for years. Down here the Spider Ichor binds it where blood once did, and the Royal Apothecary Society assures me it is safe for the living, which is more than they say about most things. The living come back for seconds, the only review I trust. Poach the links before they see the pan. A sausage that splits was packed by someone in a hurry." },
    [3397] = { "The bears of the south are bigger than ours, and their meat is darker, with a smell of pine and wet earth that the hot spice cuts through. Sear the steak until it smells of smoke and iron, then let it rest a long while. A big steak goes on cooking after the fire, and many a cook has ruined one by staring at it. I tell my students to walk away and count to a hundred. Most reach forty." },
    [3398] = { "The lions of the Barrens are lean and sweet close to the bone, so the chops stay on the bone. Hot spice, a moderate fire, and many turns. I cannot tell you how hot they come out. I can tell you the orcs from the Crossroads ask for more spice every time and the trolls ask for less, and I make two pans and let them argue about who has the right one." },
    [3399] = { "Lion cooked as a steak rather than chopped, with soothing spice to round the sweetness of the meat. The soothing spice smells of cloves and warm stone. Rub it in, sear the steak hard, rest it as long as you seared it. Slice it across the grain, or it fights back in the mouth. The name was written by someone who had eaten it and was too busy to find a better word." },
    [4094] = { "There are more buzzards than cooks in the Barrens, and the cooks have found a use for them. The wing wants hot spice, a long low heat to soften the sinew, then a short fierce flame to blister the skin. You can hear the change. The fat stops whispering and starts to crackle. Serve them hot and in quantity. Nobody has ever eaten only one buzzard wing." },
    [6412] = { "An elven recipe, from a tree so tall the night elves are said to cook in its branches. I have never been, and I am not expected. The recipe came down to me through three traders, two of them honest. Thread the spider legs on a skewer and give them a quick, fierce heat until the shells crack like twigs in a fire. That sound is the signal. After it, every moment costs you." },
    [6413] = { "Orgrimmar's cooks sent this north on the first zeppelins, and the Undercity took to it. The surprise is in the stinger. Cooked until it turns from glass to chalk, it loses its venom and keeps a pleasant crunch. Undercooked, it keeps the venom, and the surprise is a different one. You can hear the change. A raw stinger rattles in the pan. A done one lies still." },
    [6414] = { "The tauren of Mulgore roast kodo the way they do most things, slowly and without fuss. I cook it the same way. Kodo is a great, slow animal, and its meat is tough until it decides otherwise. A pinch of spice, a low fire, and an afternoon. It is done when it smells of warm grass and smoke. A tauren trader once told me mine was nearly as good as his mother's. I chose to believe the nearly was politeness." },
    [6418] = { "The Barrens taught me this one, along with patience and a respect for anything that survives there. A thunder lizard's tail is solid muscle wrapped in a skin like old leather. Cook it in its own fat with hot spice until the skin crackles under a spoon. If it bends, it is not done. If it shatters, it is perfect. The orcs eat it whole. I cut it into rounds, for the sake of the furniture." },
    [6419] = { "There were deer in the woods above the farm, and my father, or my uncle, or a neighbour with a bow, brought one down each autumn. The face is gone. The smell of the meat in the pan is not. Venison is lean and proud and wants a great deal of spice and very little fire. Take it off while the middle is still dark. Lean meat cooked through tastes of nothing but regret." },
    [8607] = { "No spice and no sauce, only smoke and time. This is food for people who are going somewhere. Cut the meat along the grain and hang it high over a low fire of green wood, where the heat never reaches and the smoke does all the work. In the morning the strips should bend before they break. I pack them for the deathguards on the road to the Sepulcher. They do not need to eat, and they take three each." },
    [15853] = { "A plain steak from a wolf's flank, with a pinch of spice and nothing to hide behind. That is what makes it a test. Sear it fast, rest it long, slice it thin across the grain. I make my students cook it until they stop flinching at the hiss of the pan. It takes some of them a week. One took a year. He is very good now, and he still flinches, a little." },
    [15855] = { "The camps of Stranglethorn roast raptor in large pieces, and so do I, because raptor is lean and a large piece keeps its own middle moist. Hot spice on the outside, a strong fire, then pull it away from the heat to finish. The smell turns from metal to smoke to roasted nuts. Somewhere around the nuts, take it off. The raptor fought hard. Do not let it win at the table." },
    [15856] = { "Red wolves from the far south, fattier than the grey ones of Silverpine, with ribs that need time to render. Hot spice and a hot fire, then patience. The fat runs clear before it runs smoky, and the moment between is the one you want. The deathguards who come back from the south bring these up with them wrapped in cloth, as if they had found treasure. For a cook, they had." },
    [15863] = { "The trolls of the swamp call this a pie and mean it kindly. Mystery meat and hot spice, cooked until there is no doubt left about anything. I can smell what the meat was, which is more than the trolls can, and I still serve it. Cook it through, then cook it again. Whatever it was, it has had a long journey, and it deserves a firm ending." },
    [15935] = { "Every new cook in Tirisfal makes this, because the bats are everywhere and little else is. I have made more of them than any dish of my first life. Score the skin of the wing and lay it on a hot griddle, skin down, and leave it alone. It talks while it cooks. When the hiss turns to a crackle, turn it once and never again. Serve it to whoever is still hungry. In the Undercity, there is always someone." },
    [21175] = { "Spiders are the one thing the Undercity is never short of. White spider meat is sweet and delicate and takes no spice at all, which is a mercy in a city that smells of everything. Press it into casings firm enough to hold and thin enough to crack. In the pan, listen for the snap. It is the most satisfying sound in my kitchen, and my students learn to wait for it." },
    [22480] = { "The wolves of the cold places live slowly and keep their fat, and their meat is the best of any wolf. Soothing spice, a short hot sear, a long rest. You will smell when it is ready: the spice warms and the meat turns sweet, like a loaf just out of the oven. A cook who has fought a hundred stringy wolves deserves this one. I serve it to my best students, and I do not tell the others." },
    [25659] = { "A goblin in Gadgetzan cooks chimaerok with rocket fuel, and the Horde's travellers carry the recipe home like a dare. Mine came from a tauren who had lost his eyebrows and kept his appetite. Chimaerok tenderloin is a long way to go for a piece of meat, the islands off Feralas, and it is worth every step. Deeprock salt, hot spice, and the goblin rocket fuel, which goes on the fire, never in the food. Stand back further than seems polite. The flare lasts a breath and leaves a crust no honest coal could make. The smell fills the whole Apothecarium, and the apothecaries come out of their rooms to complain and stay to eat. I do not ask what they think of it. I count the plates." },
    [1225758] = { "Tiger, the name means, cut thick and rubbed with holy salts an hour before cooking. Tiger is a hard-working meat and the salt calms it. Then a hot iron, a short sear and a long rest. You will hear it relax on the board, a soft settling, like a dog lying down. That is when you slice. Not before. A tiger that has not settled will not settle at the table either." },
    [1225759] = { "Holy salts, from the churches of the living, which made me laugh the first time I held a pouch of them. They are good salts. The Light has nothing against me in a kitchen. Rub them into the red wolf flank and leave it an hour, then cook it quickly. The salt draws the water from the meat and firms it. It does not burn me, before you ask. My students always ask." },
    [1250142] = { "Small rounds of chopped stag and hot spice, pressed once and fried quickly. Press them twice and the juice runs into the pan instead of the guest. The living like food they can eat with one hand. I have watched dockworkers at the zeppelin tower finish a dozen of these between two arrivals and go back to work without a word. That is the review that matters." },
    [1250143] = { "Buzzard wings, boned and beaten flat, rolled around soothing spice and tied. It is slow, fiddly work, and I give it to students who are proud of their knife. Roast it gently and slice it in rounds. The spiral inside tells me everything about the cook who rolled it. A tight spiral, a careful hand. A loose one, someone who was thinking about lunch." },
    [1250144] = { "A poor farmer's stew of chopped meat, cooked long and slow. In Lordaeron it was beef, when there was beef, or I believe it was. Down here it is bat. Chop the wings fine, add the soothing spice, and give it a low fire for an evening, until the sinew softens and the kitchen smells of cloves and of something like home. My students call it bat hash. I call it supper." },
    [1250146] = { "There is no pepper in it, whatever the name says, only red wolf flank and soothing spice, which does pepper's work well enough. Sear the flank hard, rest it while the pan cools, then pour its juices back over it. The smell when the juices meet the hot meat again is the whole point of the dish. I serve it to the apothecaries when I want something from them, and it works." },
    [1250147] = { "Thin slices of big bear meat layered with soothing spice and roasted slowly, until the layers press together into one. Slice the meat cold, or it tears. The smell when it is done is the best part, dark and sweet and close to burnt sugar. The name means nothing to me, nor to anyone I have asked. Whoever named it was showing off. Whoever cooked it first was not." },
    [1250148] = { "Stag, rubbed deep with soothing spice and seared on a very hot iron, served at once while it still steams. The steam carries the smell, and the smell is half the meal for anyone who can taste and all of it for me. Do not let it wait. A steak that stops steaming has started to cool, and a cold steak is a promise someone broke on the way from the kitchen." },
    [1250149] = { "Tiger and soothing spice, the hottest fire you can make, the shortest time you can bear. The name is a hunter's. The method is a cook's. Strike fast, then rest long, and the resting is where everyone fails, because the smell is wonderful and the fork is right there. I make my students sit on their hands. The first time, I mean it." },
    [1250150] = { "A recipe I have read but never been sold, which makes me suspicious of it. The ribs cook low until the meat lets go of the bone, then over a fierce flame to set a crust of hot spice, and there is a great deal of it. The smoke alone made my eyes water. I cannot tell you how hot they are. I can tell you nobody I fed them to asked for more spice." },
    [1250151] = { "The toughest cut of the toughest animal, and the reason a cook learns to keep a fire low. Rub the brisket with hot spice and let it cook through a whole day, until it gives under a fork like wet bread. You cannot rush it. You can only watch it, and smell it change from raw iron to smoke to something rich and dark. When it smells like a winter evening, it is ready." },
    [1250152] = { "Raptor cooked for most of a day over the lowest fire I can keep, with a great deal of soothing spice, until it falls apart at the touch of a fork. Pull it with two forks, never a knife. The deathguards love this one because it keeps, and because it can be eaten from a bowl while walking. Prehistoric, it calls itself. So are some of my students' manners." },
    [1250153] = { "Thin slices of raptor rolled around hot spice and tied with string. The rolls cook from the outside in, so keep the outer turn thin. Brown them on every side, then lower the fire and leave them. When the string darkens and smells of burnt hay, cut it away before anyone sees it. A guest will forgive a great deal, but not string. I learned that from a guest." },
    [1250154] = { "Spiced before and after, because raptor is lean and the first coat cooks away. I learned to smell the moment between the two. When the slices stop hissing and begin to tick, like a pan cooling, take them off and season them again while they are hot enough to drink it. My students always forget the second coat. The dish tells on them. It smells of half a recipe." },
    [1250156] = { "A raptor's egg is the size of my two fists and smells, when cracked, faintly of the swamp its mother walked through. The spice is there to answer that smell, and there is a great deal of it. Beat the egg, season it, and cook it in small rounds on a hot stone. The rounds puff when they are done. My students eat them before I can count them, which tells me the recipe works." },
    [1295834] = { "Boar, roasted on the bone with a little mild spice and nothing else, the way farm kitchens have always done it. The recipe comes from the Riverglades, from someone who clearly had hungry people waiting. Roast it until the fat crackles and the smell turns from meat to hazelnut. Do not decorate it. A plain dish is a promise that the meat is good, and a cook who adds things is admitting the meat was not." },
  },
}
-- (the places Classic Era's client names otherwise, swapped in there)
